<div class="figma-page-content" style="font-family: Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; line-height: 1.62; color: #0f172a; max-width: 1080px; margin: 0 auto;">
<h1 class="figma-screen-title">The Basic HTML Document Structure</h1>
<div class="figma-reading-card">
<p style="font-size: 17px;">A complete HTML page follows a specific document structure. Before there are any major tags, you must put "<span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</span>". This tells the browser to use modern HTML. This is a tag. Last submodule, you learnt all about tags. Unlike the previous tag structure, this is a standalone tag, which don't require a closing tag. Some other standalone tags include:</p>
<ul>
<li style="font-size: 17px;"><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">&lt;br&gt;</span> - Placed in paragraphs or other large text clumps in order to start a new line.</li>
<li style="font-size: 17px;"><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">&lt;hr&gt;</span> - Makes a horizontal line to break sections up.</li>
<li style="font-size: 17px;"><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">&lt;img&gt;</span> - Allows you to place an image in your document.</li>
</ul>
<p style="font-size: 17px;">Earlier, we discussed the html tag. The <span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">&lt;html&gt;</span> tag wraps the whole document. Inside it, the <span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">&lt;head&gt;</span> stores information about the page, like <span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">&lt;link&gt;</span> tags and the <span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">&lt;title&gt;</span>. An snippet of an HTML head should look like this:</p>
<hr />
<p style="font-size: 17px;"><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">&lt;head&gt;<br />    &lt;meta charset="UTF-8"&gt;<br /></span><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">    &lt;title&gt;My Website&lt;/title&gt;</span><br /><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">    &lt;link rel="icon" type="png" href="my_favicon.png" /&gt;</span><br /><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">&lt;head&gt;</span></p>
<hr />
<p>The <span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">&lt;title&gt;</span> inside the head names the browser tab. The character-set meta element helps the browser interpret text correctly, and the viewport meta element helps the page scale on phones and tablets. The &lt;link&gt; provides a favicon, which is an image / icon next to the title. These lines are not visually exciting, but they are part of a dependable starting template. Notice also that most HTML elements have an opening tag and a closing tag, and nested elements are indented so their relationships are easier to read.</p>
<p>HTML describes meaning before appearance. At this stage, the page may look plain because CSS has not been added. That is okay. Your first goal is a correctly organized document that the browser can understand. Styling comes after the structure is in place. HTML can be seen as the skeleton of the document, meaning that as long as it is there, there is something the browser can recognize.</p>
<p>The <span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">&lt;body&gt;</span> element stores what's meant to be visible. For example, paragraphs and headers are put there. They are also found in this nested format. You may take a look below in the provided code editor.</p>
</div>
<div class="figma-info-grid">
<div style="flex: 1 1 440px; background: #f8fafc; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 13px; padding: 17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size: 20px; margin: 0 0 8px;">Key ideas</h2>
<ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 22px;">
<li>Put page information in <span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">&lt;head&gt;</span>.</li>
<li>Put visible page content in <span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">&lt;body&gt;</span>.</li>
<li>Nest tags carefully and keep indentation consistent.<br /></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div style="flex: 1 1 360px; background: #ecfeff; border: 1px solid #99f6e4; border-radius: 13px; padding: 17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size: 20px; margin: 0 0 8px; color: #115e59;">Your task</h2>
<p style="margin: 0;">In the editor, add something to head, then add something to body. See how it changes the document and what it does.</p>
</div>
</div>
