<div class="figma-page-content" style="font-family: Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; line-height: 1.62; color: #0f172a; max-width: 1080px; margin: 0 auto;">
<h1 class="figma-screen-title">Some Extra Information</h1>
<div class="figma-reading-card">
<p>These activities should give you a grasp on how HTML should be structured. You may practice more using the code editor above or the code editor on the next page.</p>
<p>One more important thing to note is a few things about HTML that have been either seen or will be seen from now on.<br /></p>
<ul>
<li>Elements and tags can have attributes. It typically looks like &lt;tag attribute="setting"&gt; ... &lt;/tag&gt;. One example of this is in an &lt;img&gt; tag, you can set a properties like src which is the image's file name and alt that gives a description. Here is an example of this in action: <span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">&lt;img scr="Chart.png" alt="A picture of a chart"&gt;</span></li>
<li>HTML does have comments. Any text between '<span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">&lt;!--</span>' and '<span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">--&gt;</span>' will be seen as a comment and not rendered by the Browser.</li>
<li>One more important thing is the anchor tag. You can put the anchor tag around something to make it a link to something else. This can be anything, from images, to paragraphs, to divs. An example of this is: <span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">&lt;a href="MyOtherPage.html"&gt;&lt;p&gt;Click this to go to my other page!&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</span>.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<details style="margin: 22px 0; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 12px; padding: 14px 16px; background: white;">
<summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: bold; color: #0f766e;">Quick check: Where should a visible paragraph normally be placed?</summary>
<p style="margin: 12px 0 0;">Inside the &lt;body&gt; element.</p>
</details>
