<div class="figma-page-content" style="font-family: Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; line-height: 1.62; color: #0f172a; max-width: 1080px; margin: 0 auto;">
<h1 class="figma-screen-title">An Introduction to Styles</h1>
<div class="figma-reading-card">
<p style="font-size: 17px;">From the previous sections, it can be seen on how HTML can set up a page. However, these setups typically lack colors, layout, and styles. That's because styling is the job of CSS, which stands for Cascading Style Sheet. The reason it is called this is because the page is a sheet composed of many style rules that 'cascade' in a way, with broad rules being overtaken by more specific rules.</p>
<p style="font-size: 17px;">In this submodule, you will learn some basic CSS rules, properties, and values, and then show how to connect to HTML. More advanced items will come in the following pages / submodules. First though, let's go over how a CSS should look like.</p>
<p style="font-size: 17px;">The following is an example of how CSS is written:</p>
<hr />
<p><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">* {<br />    color: green;<br />}<br />p {</span><br /><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">    color: blue;</span><br /><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">    font-family: Arial;</span><br /><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">    font-size: 12px;</span><br /><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">}</span><br /><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">.className {</span><br /><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">    border: solid 1px red;</span><br /><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">    display: box;</span><br /><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">}</span><br /><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">#idName {</span><br /><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">    color: black;</span><br /><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">    font-size: 20px;<br /></span><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;"></span><span style="font-family: 'courier new', courier, monospace;">}<br />h1 {<br />    color: pink !important;<br />}<br /></span></p>
<hr />
<p>That's an example of how CSS interacts with HTML. In the above example, it sets the color of all things like text green, then it makes all paragraph items blue, Arial, and 12px in font size. It then styles all elements with the class 'className' to have red border and be a box. Afterwards, it then styles an element with the id "idName". Finally, it declares something a rule as important.</p>
<p>For now, the rule of thumb is that the important flag is the most specific, and will take the most priority, then ids, then classes, then general items, then finally, with the lowest priority being Universal / General Rules.</p>
<p>Below will be more properties and values, however, they should be obvious based off the name of them alone. In addition, some of them will be things you've seen from the example above.</p>
</div>
<div class="figma-info-grid">
<div style="flex: 1 1 440px; background: #f8fafc; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 13px; padding: 17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size: 20px; margin: 0 0 8px;">Key ideas</h2>
<ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 22px;">
<li>What is CSS for.</li>
<li>How CSS works, and what CSS does.</li>
<li>How CSS connects to HTML.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div style="flex: 1 1 360px; background: #ecfeff; border: 1px solid #99f6e4; border-radius: 13px; padding: 17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size: 20px; margin: 0 0 8px; color: #115e59;">Your task</h2>
<p style="margin: 0;">Knowing what you know about CSS so far, complete the activity below and continue to the next text.</p>
</div>
</div>
