<div class="figma-page-content" style="font-family: Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif; line-height: 1.62; color: #0f172a; max-width: 1080px; margin: 0 auto;">
<h1 class="figma-screen-title">What JavaScript Adds to the Page</h1>
<div class="figma-reading-card">
<p style="font-size: 17px;">The webpage already has structure and visual design, but it behaves mostly like a document. JavaScript adds the ability to respond after the page loads. It can react to a click, read information from a form, change text, add or remove a CSS class, or perform calculations. This module uses one small interaction so you can understand the connection without turning the project into a large programming exercise.</p>
<p>JavaScript works with values and instructions. A const declaration can store a reference that will not be reassigned, while let can store a value that needs to change later. You will not need advanced data structures here. The important idea is that JavaScript can locate an HTML element through the browser's Document Object Model, commonly called the DOM, and then read or change that element.</p>
<p>The project will add a practice card containing a paragraph and button. HTML will create those elements, CSS will style them, and JavaScript will change the paragraph after the button is selected. The behavior is intentionally simple because the learning objective is to understand the relationship among the technologies.</p>
</div>
<div class="figma-info-grid">
<div style="flex: 1 1 440px; background: #f8fafc; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 13px; padding: 17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size: 20px; margin: 0 0 8px;">Key ideas</h2>
<ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 22px;">
<li>JavaScript adds behavior after the page loads.</li>
<li>The DOM is the browser's representation of the HTML page.</li>
<li>JavaScript can select an element and change its content.</li>
<li>Keep beginner interactions small enough to explain clearly.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div style="flex: 1 1 360px; background: #ecfeff; border: 1px solid #99f6e4; border-radius: 13px; padding: 17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size: 20px; margin: 0 0 8px; color: #115e59;">Your task</h2>
<p style="margin: 0;">Read the new practice-card HTML. Identify the paragraph id and button id that JavaScript will use later. Do not worry about writing the event yet.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div style="margin: 22px 0;"><iframe width="100" height="680" style="width: 100%; height: 680px; border: 0; border-radius: 14px; background: #f8fafc; display: block;" class="figma-code-lab" loading="lazy" sandbox="allow-scripts allow-forms allow-modals allow-popups" src="{{context_path}}/content/resources/embeds/screen-11-editor-01.html" title="Interactive code editor: JavaScript Orientation"></iframe></div>
<details style="margin: 22px 0; border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 12px; padding: 14px 16px; background: white;">
<summary style="cursor: pointer; font-weight: bold; color: #0f766e;">Quick check: What new capability does JavaScript add to this project?</summary>
<p style="margin: 12px 0 0;">It allows the page to respond to a user's action and change content.</p>
</details>
