<div class="figma-page-content" style="font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;line-height:1.62;color:#0f172a;max-width:1080px;margin:0 auto;">
<h1 class="figma-screen-title">Understand Simple Events</h1>
<div class="figma-reading-card"><p style="font-size:17px;">An event is something that happens in the page, such as a click, key press, input change, or form submission. JavaScript can listen for an event and run a function when it occurs. For the course project, the important event is a button click. The browser waits until the visitor selects the button, then runs the instructions associated with that click.</p><p>The addEventListener method is a common way to connect an element to an event. Its first argument names the event, such as "click", and the second argument is the function that should run. The function is sometimes called a callback because the browser calls it later when the event happens. This separates loading the page from responding to user behavior.</p><p>In the editor, the button and paragraph already exist in HTML. CSS already controls how the button looks. Your JavaScript will connect behavior to the existing structure. This division is important: HTML does not need an entirely new button just because you want new behavior, and JavaScript does not need to define every color or spacing value.</p></div>
<div class="figma-info-grid" style="">
<div style="flex:1 1 440px;background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:13px;padding:17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:0 0 8px;">Key ideas</h2><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:22px;"><li>Events represent user or browser actions.</li><li>addEventListener connects an event to a callback.</li><li>A click handler runs when the user activates the element.</li><li>Keep appearance in CSS and behavior in JavaScript when possible.</li></ul>
</div>
<div style="flex:1 1 360px;background:#ecfeff;border:1px solid #99f6e4;border-radius:13px;padding:17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:0 0 8px;color:#115e59;">Your task</h2><p style="margin:0;">Run the example and click the button. Then change only the message inside the callback and run it again. Confirm that the page responds with your new text.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div style="margin:22px 0;"><iframe loading="lazy" sandbox="allow-scripts allow-forms allow-modals allow-popups" src="{{context_path}}/content/resources/embeds/screen-12-editor-01.html" style="width:100%;height:680px;border:0;border-radius:14px;background:#f8fafc;display:block;" title="Interactive code editor: JavaScript Events"></iframe></div>
<details style="margin:22px 0;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:12px;padding:14px 16px;background:white;">
<summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;color:#0f766e;">Quick check: When does the callback in a click listener run?</summary>
<p style="margin:12px 0 0;">It runs when the selected element receives the click event.</p>
</details>
