<div class="figma-page-content" style="font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;line-height:1.62;color:#0f172a;max-width:1080px;margin:0 auto;">
<h1 class="figma-screen-title">Review How HTML, CSS, and JavaScript Connect</h1>
<div class="figma-reading-card"><p style="font-size:17px;">You have now worked with each technology separately. The next step is to see the project as a connected system. HTML supplies the content and useful hooks, CSS selects that HTML and defines visual rules, and JavaScript selects HTML through the DOM and responds to events. The finished webpage feels unified even though the three layers have different responsibilities.</p><p>Consider the practice button. HTML creates the &lt;button&gt; and gives it the id tipButton. CSS selects button elements and controls padding, colors, borders, and hover behavior. JavaScript uses the id to find that same button and attaches a click listener. When the learner clicks, JavaScript updates the paragraph that HTML created. The browser redraws the page using the existing CSS rules.</p><p>This separation helps debugging. If the button is missing completely, inspect the HTML. If it exists but looks wrong, inspect the CSS. If it looks correct but does nothing, inspect the JavaScript and the IDs that connect it to the HTML. Starting with the layer responsible for the symptom is more efficient than changing all three at once.</p></div>
<div class="figma-info-grid" style="">
<div style="flex:1 1 440px;background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:13px;padding:17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:0 0 8px;">Key ideas</h2><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:22px;"><li>HTML answers: what is on the page?</li><li>CSS answers: how does it look and arrange itself?</li><li>JavaScript answers: what happens when the user interacts?</li><li>Debug by matching the symptom to the responsible layer.</li></ul>
</div>
<div style="flex:1 1 360px;background:#ecfeff;border:1px solid #99f6e4;border-radius:13px;padding:17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:0 0 8px;color:#115e59;">Your task</h2><p style="margin:0;">Use the connection map, then explain the button to yourself in three sentences: one for HTML, one for CSS, and one for JavaScript.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div style="margin:22px 0;"><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr auto 1fr auto 1fr;align-items:center;gap:10px;margin:20px 0;">
<div style="padding:18px;border-radius:12px;background:#ecfeff;border:2px solid #0f766e;text-align:center;"><strong>HTML</strong><br/><span>Structure &amp; content</span></div>
<div aria-hidden="true" style="font-size:28px;text-align:center;">→</div>
<div style="padding:18px;border-radius:12px;background:#eff6ff;border:2px solid #2563eb;text-align:center;"><strong>CSS</strong><br/><span>Appearance &amp; layout</span></div>
<div aria-hidden="true" style="font-size:28px;text-align:center;">→</div>
<div style="padding:18px;border-radius:12px;background:#fff7ed;border:2px solid #b45309;text-align:center;"><strong>JavaScript</strong><br/><span>Behavior &amp; interaction</span></div>
</div></div>
<details style="margin:22px 0;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:12px;padding:14px 16px;background:white;">
<summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;color:#0f766e;">Quick check: If the button is visible and styled correctly but never responds to a click, which layer should you inspect first?</summary>
<p style="margin:12px 0 0;">JavaScript, especially the element selection and event listener.</p>
</details>
<div class="figma-footer-line" style="padding-top:13px;border-top:1px solid #e2e8f0;color:#64748b;font-size:13px;">Basics in Webpage Making · Screen 15 of 20</div>
