<div class="figma-page-content" style="font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;line-height:1.62;color:#0f172a;max-width:1080px;margin:0 auto;">
<h1 class="figma-screen-title">Build the Final Webpage: Styling and Interaction</h1>
<div class="figma-reading-card"><p style="font-size:17px;">Now add the final two layers to the HTML you just reviewed. The stylesheet provides the visual system: a responsive header, readable content width, cards, spacing, image behavior, and button styling. JavaScript provides the interaction: selecting the practice button updates the message and changes the button label. Each layer improves the experience without replacing the work of the others.</p><p>Use the editor to make at least one intentional change in each pane. In HTML, personalize one sentence. In CSS, change the accent color or spacing. In JavaScript, rewrite the practice-tip message. Then test the button and shrink the browser width. Your finished page should still be readable, the button should still be easy to identify, and the interaction should produce visible feedback.</p><p>Avoid adding complexity only to make the page seem more advanced. The proposal calls for a basic webpage and one simple interactive feature. A polished beginner project with clear code, consistent design, and dependable behavior better demonstrates the learning objectives than a large feature that is difficult to explain or test.</p></div>
<div class="figma-info-grid" style="">
<div style="flex:1 1 440px;background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:13px;padding:17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:0 0 8px;">Key ideas</h2><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:22px;"><li>Make one controlled change in each language.</li><li>Test both appearance and behavior.</li><li>Check the layout at a narrow width.</li><li>Prefer a clear, dependable interaction over unnecessary complexity.</li></ul>
</div>
<div style="flex:1 1 360px;background:#ecfeff;border:1px solid #99f6e4;border-radius:13px;padding:17px 19px;">
<h2 style="font-size:20px;margin:0 0 8px;color:#115e59;">Your task</h2><p style="margin:0;">Complete the three-layer final build: personalize HTML, adjust one CSS design choice, and rewrite the JavaScript feedback message.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div style="margin:22px 0;"><iframe loading="lazy" sandbox="allow-scripts allow-forms allow-modals allow-popups" src="{{context_path}}/content/resources/embeds/screen-17-editor-01.html" style="width:100%;height:680px;border:0;border-radius:14px;background:#f8fafc;display:block;" title="Interactive code editor: Final Build: HTML + CSS + JavaScript"></iframe></div>
<details style="margin:22px 0;border:1px solid #cbd5e1;border-radius:12px;padding:14px 16px;background:white;">
<summary style="cursor:pointer;font-weight:700;color:#0f766e;">Quick check: What makes this a combined webpage rather than three separate exercises?</summary>
<p style="margin:12px 0 0;">The CSS and JavaScript both operate on the same HTML structure to produce one final user experience.</p>
</details>
<div class="figma-footer-line" style="padding-top:13px;border-top:1px solid #e2e8f0;color:#64748b;font-size:13px;">Basics in Webpage Making · Screen 17 of 20</div>
